<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- MATH 14 - Review Problems #3, practice version: the same problems  -->
<!-- as review3-online.ptx, but with every <solution> removed and every  -->
<!-- <feedback> reduced to a neutral right/wrong message, so students     -->
<!-- can check their answers without seeing anything about the solutions. -->
<!-- This file is GENERATED-STYLE but maintained by hand: if the problems -->
<!-- in review3-online.ptx change, regenerate or mirror the change here. -->
<section xml:id="sec-exercises-review3-practice" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Review Problems #3</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Sections: <xref ref="section-mass-center-of-mass" text="title"/>, <xref ref="section-jacobian-substitution" text="title"/>,
      <xref ref="section-surface-integrals" text="title"/>, and
      <xref ref="section-greens-theorem" text="title"/>. Work each
      problem, then type your answers into the boxes and have them checked
      here.
    </p>
    <p>
      Press <q>Check Me</q> to submit; you may check as often as you like.
    </p>
    <p>
      Checking an answer tells you whether it is right or wrong and
      nothing more<mdash/>use the boxes to test yourself. A complete
      worked solution for every problem will be revealed beneath it
      once solutions are posted.
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>How to type your answers.</term>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Give exact answers, not decimals.</term> A rounded decimal
          will be marked wrong, even to several places. Type the exact
          value: <c>pi*8^3/2</c> rather than <c>1608.495</c>, and
          <c>4*5/(3*pi)</c> rather than <c>2.122</c>. A whole number or an
          exact fraction may of course be typed as it stands, so
          <c>77</c> and <c>7/3</c> are both fine.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>How to type it.</term> Write products with <c>*</c> and
          powers with <c>^</c>, and write functions with parentheses:
          <c>sqrt(s-t)</c>, <c>ln(9)</c>, <c>exp(-4)</c>. Use <c>pi</c> for
          <m>\pi</m>. So <m>\dfrac{u^2}{v}</m> is typed <c>u^2/v</c>, and
          <m>2\pi\left(e^{-1} - e^{-4}\right)</m> is typed
          <c>2*pi*(exp(-1)-exp(-4))</c>. Spaces are ignored.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Any equivalent form counts.</term> Your answer is compared
          to the exact value, not to one particular way of writing it, so
          <c>2*pi*exp(-1)-2*pi*exp(-4)</c> is accepted just as readily as
          <c>2*pi*(exp(-1)-exp(-4))</c>. Do not simplify on our
          account<mdash/>just do not round.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Words.</term> Type just the word or letters asked for, in
          lower case unless the problem says otherwise.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </introduction>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-review3-practice">

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 1.  Area by a non-linear change of variables  (RP3 #1)              -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-area-change-of-variables">
    <title>Area by a Non-Linear Change of Variables</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="5" max="7" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="aa" context="number">
        <de-expression>a^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="bb" context="number">
        <de-expression>b^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="one" context="number">
        <de-expression>1</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xuv" context="formula">
        <de-expression>u^2/v</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="yuv" context="formula">
        <de-expression>v/u</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xu" context="formula">
        <de-expression>2*u/v</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xv" context="formula">
        <de-expression>-u^2/v^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="yu" context="formula">
        <de-expression>-v/u^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="yv" context="formula">
        <de-expression>1/u</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="jac" context="formula">
        <de-expression>1/v</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="area" context="number">
        <de-expression>(aa-1)*ln(bb)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the area of the region <m>D</m> in the first quadrant of the
        <m>xy</m>-plane bounded by
      </p>
      <me>
        xy = 1, \qquad xy = <eval obj="aa"/>, \qquad
        xy^2 = 1, \qquad xy^2 = <eval obj="bb"/>.
      </me>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Sketch the region <m>D</m>. (Nothing to submit
        for this part<mdash/>the sketch is for your own use in the parts
        that follow.)
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Using the non-linear change of variables
        <m>u = xy</m> and <m>v = xy^2</m>, find <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> as
        functions of <m>u</m> and <m>v</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>x = x(u,v) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="xuv" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>y = y(u,v) =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="yuv" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part C.</term> Find the four partial derivatives in the
        Jacobian matrix of this change of variables, and then its
        determinant.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial x}{\partial u} =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="xu" width="12"/>,
        <m>\dfrac{\partial x}{\partial v} =</m>
        <fillin name="b4" mode="math" ansobj="xv" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial y}{\partial u} =</m>
        <fillin name="b5" mode="math" ansobj="yu" width="12"/>,
        <m>\dfrac{\partial y}{\partial v} =</m>
        <fillin name="b6" mode="math" ansobj="yv" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\left| \dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)} \right| =</m>
        <fillin name="b7" mode="math" ansobj="jac" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part D.</term> Using the change of variables, set up a double
        integral for the area of <m>D</m> in the form
        <m>\displaystyle\int_{c}^{d}\!\int_{p}^{q}</m>
        <fillin name="b8" mode="math" ansobj="jac" width="12"/>
        <m>\,du\,dv</m>, where
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>c =</m> <fillin name="b9" mode="math" ansobj="one" width="5"/>,
        <m>d =</m> <fillin name="b10" mode="math" ansobj="bb" width="5"/>,
        <m>p =</m> <fillin name="b11" mode="math" ansobj="one" width="5"/>, and
        <m>q =</m> <fillin name="b12" mode="math" ansobj="aa" width="5"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part E.</term> Evaluate the double integral to find the area of
        <m>D</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Area <m>=</m>
        <fillin name="b13" mode="math" ansobj="area" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Dividing <m>v = xy^2</m> by <m>u = xy</m> gives <m>y = v/u</m>, and
        then <m>x = u/y = u^2/v</m>. The Jacobian matrix has entries
        <m>x_u = 2u/v</m>, <m>x_v = -u^2/v^2</m>, <m>y_u = -v/u^2</m>,
        <m>y_v = 1/u</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)}
        = \frac{2u}{v}\cdot\frac{1}{u}
        - \left(-\frac{u^2}{v^2}\right)\left(-\frac{v}{u^2}\right)
        = \frac{2}{v} - \frac{1}{v} = \frac{1}{v}.
      </me>
      <p>
        The four bounding curves are exactly <m>u = 1</m>,
        <m>u = 9</m>, <m>v = 1</m>, <m>v = 36</m>,
        so <m>D</m> becomes a rectangle and
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{Area}
        = \int_1^{36}\!\!\int_1^{9}
          \frac{1}{v}\,du\,dv
        = \left(9 - 1\right)
          \ln\!\left(36\right).
      </me>
      <p>
        Here the four curves are <m>u = 1</m>, <m>u = 9</m>, <m>v = 1</m> and
<m>v = 36</m>, so the area is
<m>(9-1)\ln 36 = 8\ln 36 \approx 28.67</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 2.  Area of a tilted ellipse by a shear  (RP3 #2)                   -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-shear-ellipse-area">
    <title>Area of a Tilted Ellipse by a Shear</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="twoa" context="number">
        <de-expression>2*a</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="bcoef" context="number">
        <de-expression>a^2+1</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xst" context="formula">
        <de-expression>s-a*t</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="yst" context="formula">
        <de-expression>t</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="jac" context="number">
        <de-expression>1</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="area" context="number">
        <de-expression>pi</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the change of variables
        <m>s = x + <eval obj="a"/>y</m>, <m>t = y</m> to find the area of
        the region bounded by
      </p>
      <me>
        x^2 + <eval obj="twoa"/>xy + <eval obj="bcoef"/>y^2 = 1 .
      </me>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Invert the change of variables.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>x = x(s,t) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="xst" width="12"/>,
        <m>y = y(s,t) =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="yst" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Find the Jacobian determinant
        <m>\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(s,t)}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(s,t)} =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="jac" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part C.</term> Find the area of the region.
      </p>
      <p>
        Area <m>=</m>
        <fillin name="b4" mode="math" ansobj="area" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Inverting the shear gives <m>x = s - 3t</m>,
        <m>y = t</m>, so the Jacobian matrix is
        <m>\begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; -3 \\ 0 &amp; 1
        \end{pmatrix}</m> with determinant <m>1</m>, and
        <m>dx\,dy = ds\,dt</m>. Completing the substitution,
      </p>
      <me>
        x^2 + 6xy + 10y^2
        = \left(s - 3t\right)^2
        + 6\left(s - 3t\right)t
        + 10t^2
        = s^2 + t^2 ,
      </me>
      <p>
        so the region is the unit disc <m>T</m> and
        <m>\iint_R dx\,dy = \iint_T ds\,dt = \text{Area}(T) = \pi</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 3</m>, and the area is <m>\pi</m> whatever the value of
<m>a</m>: the shear does not change area.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 3.  A double integral under s = y, t = y - x^2  (RP3 #3)            -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-parabolic-strip">
    <title>A Double Integral Under a Parabolic Change of Variables</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="bsq" context="number">
        <de-expression>b^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="nega" context="number">
        <de-expression>-a</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="zero" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xst" context="formula">
        <de-expression>sqrt(s-t)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="yst" context="formula">
        <de-expression>s</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="jac" context="formula">
        <de-expression>1/(2*sqrt(s-t))</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="integrand" context="formula">
        <de-expression>1/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="value" context="number">
        <de-expression>a*bsq/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the change of variables <m>s = y</m>, <m>t = y - x^2</m> to
        evaluate <m>\displaystyle\iint_R x\,dx\,dy</m> over the region
        <m>R</m> in the first quadrant bounded by <m>y = 0</m>,
        <m>y = <eval obj="bsq"/></m>, <m>y = x^2</m>, and
        <m>y = x^2 - <eval obj="a"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Invert the change of variables. (Take the
        positive square root, since <m>R</m> lies in the first quadrant.)
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>x = x(s,t) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="xst" width="14"/>,
        <m>y = y(s,t) =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="yst" width="10"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Find the Jacobian determinant.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(s,t)} =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="jac" width="16"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part C.</term> The region <m>R</m> becomes a rectangle
        <m>T</m> in the <m>st</m>-plane. Give its limits.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>s</m> runs from
        <fillin name="b4" mode="math" ansobj="zero" width="5"/> to
        <fillin name="b5" mode="math" ansobj="bsq" width="5"/>, and
        <m>t</m> runs from
        <fillin name="b6" mode="math" ansobj="nega" width="5"/> to
        <fillin name="b7" mode="math" ansobj="zero" width="5"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part D.</term> After the substitution, the integrand
        <m>x</m> times the Jacobian simplifies to a constant. What is it?
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>x \left|\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(s,t)}\right| =</m>
        <fillin name="b8" mode="math" ansobj="integrand" width="8"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part E.</term> Evaluate the integral.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_R x\,dx\,dy =</m>
        <fillin name="b9" mode="math" ansobj="value" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Inverting gives <m>x = \sqrt{s-t}</m> and <m>y = s</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(s,t)}
        = \begin{vmatrix}
          \frac{1}{2\sqrt{s-t}} &amp; -\frac{1}{2\sqrt{s-t}} \\
          1 &amp; 0
          \end{vmatrix}
        = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{s-t}} .
      </me>
      <p>
        The boundaries <m>y = x^2</m> and <m>y = x^2 - 4</m>
        become <m>t = 0</m> and <m>t = -4</m>, while
        <m>y = 0</m> and <m>y = 9</m> become <m>s = 0</m>
        and <m>s = 9</m>. The integrand times the Jacobian
        collapses to <m>\sqrt{s-t}\cdot\frac{1}{2\sqrt{s-t}}
        = \frac{1}{2}</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_R x\,dx\,dy
        = \iint_T \frac{1}{2}\,ds\,dt
        = \frac{1}{2}\,\text{Area}(T)
        = \frac{\left(4\right)\left(9\right)}{2}
        = 18 .
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 4</m> and <m>b^2 = 9</m>, so the rectangle <m>T</m> is
<m>4 \times 9</m> and the integral equals
<m>\dfrac{(4)(9)}{2} = 18</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 4.  Triple integral over an ellipsoid  (RP3 #4)                     -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-ellipsoid-integral">
    <title>A Triple Integral Over an Ellipsoid</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="jac" context="number">
        <de-expression>a*b*c</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="value" context="number">
        <de-expression>4*a*b*c*pi/5</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate the triple integral
      </p>
      <me>
        \iiint_{E} \left(\frac{x^2}{<eval obj="a"/>^2}
        + \frac{y^2}{<eval obj="b"/>^2}
        + \frac{z^2}{<eval obj="c"/>^2}\right) dx\,dy\,dz ,
      </me>
      <p>
        where <m>E</m> is the region bounded by the ellipsoid
        <m>\dfrac{x^2}{<eval obj="a"/>^2}
        + \dfrac{y^2}{<eval obj="b"/>^2}
        + \dfrac{z^2}{<eval obj="c"/>^2} = 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Use the change of variables
        <m>x = <eval obj="a"/>u</m>, <m>y = <eval obj="b"/>v</m>,
        <m>z = <eval obj="c"/>w</m>, which carries <m>E</m> to the unit
        ball. What is the Jacobian determinant
        <m>\dfrac{\partial(x,y,z)}{\partial(u,v,w)}</m>?
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial(x,y,z)}{\partial(u,v,w)} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="jac" width="8"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Evaluate the triple integral.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iiint_{E}
        \left(\frac{x^2}{<eval obj="a"/>^2}
        + \frac{y^2}{<eval obj="b"/>^2}
        + \frac{z^2}{<eval obj="c"/>^2}\right) dx\,dy\,dz =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="value" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Setting <m>x = 2u</m>, <m>y = 3v</m>,
        <m>z = 4w</m> carries <m>E</m> to the unit ball and
        has Jacobian <m>24</m>, while the integrand becomes
        <m>u^2+v^2+w^2 = \rho^2</m>. Hence
      </p>
      <me>
        \iiint_E \left(\frac{x^2}{2^2}
        + \frac{y^2}{3^2}
        + \frac{z^2}{4^2}\right) dV
        = 24\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi}\!\!\int_0^{1}
          \rho^4 \sin\phi \,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
        = \frac{4\,\pi\,24}{5} .
      </me>
      <p>
        Here the Jacobian is <m>(2)(3)(4) = 24</m>, so the integral is
<m>\dfrac{4\pi(24)}{5} = \dfrac{96\pi}{5} \approx 60.32</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 5.  Centre of mass of a tetrahedron  (RP3 #5)                       -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-tetrahedron-com">
    <title>Centre of Mass of a Tetrahedron</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="k" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="15" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="mass" context="number">
        <de-expression>8*k/105</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xcm" context="number">
        <de-expression>2/9</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="ycm" context="number">
        <de-expression>2/9</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="zcm" context="number">
        <de-expression>1/3</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the centre of mass of the solid <m>W</m> bounded by the planes
        <m>x + y + z = 1</m>, <m>x = 0</m>, <m>y = 0</m>, and <m>z = 0</m>,
        assuming a mass density of
        <m>\rho(x,y,z) = <eval obj="k"/>\sqrt{z}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Find the mass of the solid.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iiint_W <eval obj="k"/>\sqrt{z}\,dV =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="mass" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Find the coordinates of the centre of mass.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>x_{\mathrm{CM}} =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="xcm" width="10"/>,
        <m>y_{\mathrm{CM}} =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="ycm" width="10"/>,
        <m>z_{\mathrm{CM}} =</m>
        <fillin name="b4" mode="math" ansobj="zcm" width="10"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Describe <m>W</m> by <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 1-x</m>,
        <m>0 \le z \le 1-x-y</m>. Then
      </p>
      <me>
        \iiint_W 6\sqrt{z}\,dV
        = \frac{8\left(6\right)}{105}, \qquad
        \iiint_W 6x\sqrt{z}\,dV
        = \frac{16\left(6\right)}{945}, \qquad
        \iiint_W 6z\sqrt{z}\,dV
        = \frac{8\left(6\right)}{315},
      </me>
      <p>
        and the <m>y</m>-numerator equals the <m>x</m>-numerator by the
        symmetry of <m>W</m> and of <m>\rho</m> in <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
        Dividing,
      </p>
      <me>
        \left(x_{\mathrm{CM}}, y_{\mathrm{CM}}, z_{\mathrm{CM}}\right)
        = \left(\frac29, \frac29, \frac13\right),
      </me>
      <p>
        independently of the constant <m>6</m>, which cancels
        from every ratio.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>k = 6</m>, so the mass is <m>\dfrac{8(6)}{105} = \dfrac{16}{35}
\approx 0.457</m>, and the centre of mass is
<m>\left(\tfrac29, \tfrac29, \tfrac13\right)</m>, independent of
<m>k</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 6.  Mass and centre of mass of a lamina  (RP3 #6)                   -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-lamina-rectangle">
    <title>Mass and Centre of Mass of a Rectangular Lamina</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="A" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="B" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="C" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="D" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="E" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="mass" context="number">
        <de-expression>A^2*B*C/2+B^2*D*A/2+E*A*B</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xmom" context="number">
        <de-expression>C*A^3*B/3+D*A^2*B^2/4+E*A^2*B/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="ymom" context="number">
        <de-expression>C*A^2*B^2/4+D*A*B^3/3+E*A*B^2/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xbar" context="number">
        <de-expression>xmom/mass</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="ybar" context="number">
        <de-expression>ymom/mass</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A lamina occupies the part of the rectangle
        <m>0 \le x \le <eval obj="A"/></m>,
        <m>0 \le y \le <eval obj="B"/></m>, and the density at each point is
        given by the function
        <m>\rho(x,y) = <eval obj="C"/>x + <eval obj="D"/>y + <eval obj="E"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> What is the total mass?
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>m =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="mass" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Where is the centre of mass?
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\bar{x} =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="xbar" width="12"/>,
        <m>\bar{y} =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="ybar" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Over the rectangle <m>R</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        m = \int_0^{2}\!\!\int_0^{3}
        \left(4x + 5y + 6\right)
        dy\,dx = 105 ,
      </me>
      <p>
        and the two moments are
        <m>\iint_R x\rho\,dA = 113</m> and
        <m>\iint_R y\rho\,dA = 180</m>. Dividing each by the
        mass gives <m>\bar{x} \approx 1.076</m> and
        <m>\bar{y} \approx 1.714</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>A = 2</m>, <m>B = 3</m>, <m>C = 4</m>, <m>D = 5</m> and
<m>E = 6</m>, so <m>m = 105</m>, the moments are <m>113</m> and
<m>180</m>, and
<m>\left(\bar x, \bar y\right)
= \left(\tfrac{113}{105}, \tfrac{12}{7}\right)
\approx (1.076, 1.714)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 7.  Jacobian of a linear transformation  (RP3 #7)                   -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-linear-jacobian">
    <title>The Jacobian of a Linear Transformation</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="p" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="5" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="q" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="r" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="s" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="5" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="det" context="number">
        <de-expression>p*s-q*r</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xu" context="number">
        <de-expression>p</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xv" context="number">
        <de-expression>q</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="yu" context="number">
        <de-expression>r</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="yv" context="number">
        <de-expression>s</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the transformation <m>T</m> from <m>uv</m>-coordinates to
        <m>xy</m>-coordinates given by
      </p>
      <me>
        T(u,v) = \left\langle <eval obj="p"/>u + <eval obj="q"/>v, \;
        <eval obj="r"/>u + <eval obj="s"/>v \right\rangle .
      </me>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Find the four partial derivatives.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial x}{\partial u} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="xu" width="6"/>,
        <m>\dfrac{\partial x}{\partial v} =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="xv" width="6"/>,
        <m>\dfrac{\partial y}{\partial u} =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="yu" width="6"/>,
        <m>\dfrac{\partial y}{\partial v} =</m>
        <fillin name="b4" mode="math" ansobj="yv" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Compute the Jacobian of this transformation.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)} =</m>
        <fillin name="b5" mode="math" ansobj="det" width="10"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        For a linear transformation the partial derivatives are just the
        coefficients, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathrm{Jac}(T) = \frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)}
        = \begin{vmatrix}
          7 &amp; 2 \\
          3 &amp; 8
          \end{vmatrix}
        = \left(7\right)\left(8\right)
        - \left(2\right)\left(3\right)
        = 50 ,
      </me>
      <p>
        a constant: a linear map scales every area by the same factor.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here the partial derivatives are <m>7</m>, <m>2</m>, <m>3</m> and
<m>8</m>, so the Jacobian is <m>(7)(8) - (2)(3) = 50</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 8.  The map T(u,v) = (u^2-v^2, 2uv)  (RP3 #8)                       -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-quadratic-map">
    <title>A Quadratic Map and the Integral It Simplifies</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="fuv" context="formula">
        <de-expression>u^2+v^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="jac" context="formula">
        <de-expression>4*(u^2+v^2)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="value" context="number">
        <de-expression>56*a^6/45</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The map <m>T(u,v) = \left(u^2-v^2,\; 2uv\right)</m> carries the
        triangle
        <m>D_0 = \left\{(u,v) : 0 \le v \le u \le <eval obj="a"/>\right\}</m>
        onto the domain <m>D</m> in the first quadrant bounded by
        <m>x = 0</m>, <m>y = 0</m>, and the parabola through the image of
        the edge <m>u = <eval obj="a"/></m>. Use <m>T</m> to evaluate
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_{D} \sqrt{x^2+y^2}\,dx\,dy .
      </me>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Express the integrand in terms of <m>u</m> and
        <m>v</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\sqrt{x^2+y^2} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="fuv" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Find the Jacobian of <m>T</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)} =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="jac" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part C.</term> Evaluate the integral.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{D} \sqrt{x^2+y^2}\,dx\,dy =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="value" width="14"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Since <m>x = u^2-v^2</m> and <m>y = 2uv</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        \sqrt{x^2+y^2}
        = \sqrt{\left(u^2+v^2\right)^2} = u^2+v^2, \qquad
        \frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)}
        = \begin{vmatrix} 2u &amp; -2v \\ 2v &amp; 2u \end{vmatrix}
        = 4\left(u^2+v^2\right).
      </me>
      <p>
        Therefore
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_D \sqrt{x^2+y^2}\,dx\,dy
        = 4\int_0^{2}\!\!\int_0^{u}
          \left(u^4 + 2u^2v^2 + v^4\right) dv\,du
        = 4\int_0^{2} \frac{28}{15}u^5\,du
        = \frac{56\left(2\right)^6}{45} .
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 2</m>, so the integral equals
<m>\dfrac{56\left(2^6\right)}{45} = \dfrac{3584}{45} \approx 79.64</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 9.  Centre of gravity of a region between two curves  (RP3 #9)      -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-centre-of-gravity">
    <title>Centre of Gravity of a Region Between Two Curves</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="m" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="4" max="10" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="zero" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xstar" context="number">
        <de-expression>(-m+sqrt(m^2+4*b))/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="lower" context="formula">
        <de-expression>m*x</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="upper" context="formula">
        <de-expression>b-x^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="mass" context="number">
        <de-expression>xstar^6/12+(-b-m^2/2)*xstar^4/4+b^2*xstar^2/4</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xmom" context="number">
        <de-expression>xstar^7/14+(-b-m^2/2)*xstar^5/5+b^2*xstar^3/6</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="ymom" context="number">
        <de-expression>-xstar^8/24+b*xstar^6/6-m^3*xstar^5/15-b^2*xstar^4/4+b^3*xstar^2/6</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xbar" context="number">
        <de-expression>xmom/mass</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="ybar" context="number">
        <de-expression>ymom/mass</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The region <m>R</m> is bounded by the curves
        <m>y = <eval obj="m"/>x</m>, <m>y = <eval obj="b"/> - x^2</m>, and
        the <m>y</m>-axis, and its mass density is
        <m>\delta(x,y) = xy</m>. To find the centre of gravity of <m>R</m>
        you would compute the three integrals
        <m>\displaystyle\int_c^d\!\!\int_{p(x)}^{q(x)}
        \delta(x,y)\,dy\,dx</m>,
        <m>\displaystyle\int_c^d\!\!\int_{p(x)}^{q(x)}
        x\,\delta(x,y)\,dy\,dx</m>, and
        <m>\displaystyle\int_c^d\!\!\int_{p(x)}^{q(x)}
        y\,\delta(x,y)\,dy\,dx</m>, where
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>c =</m> <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="zero" width="6"/>,
        <m>d =</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="xstar" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>p(x) =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="lower" width="12"/>,
        <m>q(x) =</m>
        <fillin name="b4" mode="math" ansobj="upper" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\int_c^d\!\!\int_{p(x)}^{q(x)}
        \delta(x,y)\,dy\,dx =</m>
        <fillin name="b5" mode="math" ansobj="mass" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\int_c^d\!\!\int_{p(x)}^{q(x)}
        x\,\delta(x,y)\,dy\,dx =</m>
        <fillin name="b6" mode="math" ansobj="xmom" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\int_c^d\!\!\int_{p(x)}^{q(x)}
        y\,\delta(x,y)\,dy\,dx =</m>
        <fillin name="b7" mode="math" ansobj="ymom" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        and finally the centre of gravity is
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\overline{x} =</m>
        <fillin name="b8" mode="math" ansobj="xbar" width="12"/>,
        <m>\overline{y} =</m>
        <fillin name="b9" mode="math" ansobj="ybar" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        The line and the parabola meet where
        <m>x^2 + 2x - 8 = 0</m>, that is at
        <m>x = 2</m>, so <m>c = 0</m> and
        <m>d = 2</m>, with
        <m>p(x) = 2x</m> and
        <m>q(x) = 8 - x^2</m>. Carrying out the three
        integrals of <m>xy</m>, <m>x^2y</m>, and <m>xy^2</m> over that
        region gives <m>\tfrac{88}{3}</m>, <m>30.476</m>,
        and <m>142.933</m> respectively, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \overline{x} = \frac{30.476}{\tfrac{88}{3}}
        \approx 1.039, \qquad
        \overline{y} = \frac{142.933}{\tfrac{88}{3}}
        \approx 4.873 .
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>m = 2</m> and <m>b = 8</m>, so <m>x^{*} = 2</m> exactly, giving
<m>c = 0</m>, <m>d = 2</m>, <m>p(x) = 2x</m>, <m>q(x) = 8 - x^2</m>, the
three integrals <m>\tfrac{88}{3} \approx 29.33</m>, <m>30.48</m> and
<m>142.93</m>, and
<m>\left(\overline{x}, \overline{y}\right) \approx (1.039, 4.873)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 10.  Centre of mass of a quarter disc  (RP3 #10)                    -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-quarter-disc-com">
    <title>Centre of Mass of a Quarter Disc</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="r" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="coord" context="number">
        <de-expression>4*r/(3*pi)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The centre of mass of the quarter disc of radius
        <m><eval obj="r"/></m> lying under
        <m>y = \sqrt{\left(<eval obj="r"/>\right)^2 - x^2}</m> for
        <m>0 \le x \le <eval obj="r"/></m>, with constant density, is a
        point <m>P = \left(\overline{x}, \overline{y}\right)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\overline{x} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="coord" width="12"/>,
        <m>\overline{y} =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="coord" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Hint.</em> Use symmetry.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        By symmetry about the line <m>y = x</m>, the centre of mass lies on
        that line, so <m>\overline{x} = \overline{y}</m>. Computing
        <m>\overline{x}</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        \overline{x}
        = \frac{\displaystyle\int_0^{3}
          x\sqrt{\left(3\right)^2-x^2}\,dx}
          {\displaystyle\frac{\pi\left(3\right)^2}{4}}
        = \frac{\left(3\right)^3/3}
          {\pi\left(3\right)^2/4}
        = \frac{4\left(3\right)}{3\pi}
        \approx \tfrac{4}{\pi} \approx 1.273 ,
      </me>
      <p>
        and <m>\overline{y}</m> has the same value.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>r = 3</m>, so
<m>\overline{x} = \overline{y} = \dfrac{4(3)}{3\pi} = \dfrac{4}{\pi}
\approx 1.273</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 11.  Flux through a paraboloid cap  (RP3 #11)                        -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-paraboloid-cap">
    <title>Flux Through a Paraboloid Cap</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="2" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="u" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="0" max="1" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="t" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-expression>(2*u-1)*t</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="h" context="number">
        <de-expression>1+a^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>b*(a^4/2+a^2)*pi</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let
        <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = z\tan\!\left(y^2\right)\mathbf{i}
        + z^3\ln\!\left(x^2+1\right)\mathbf{j}
        + <eval obj="b"/>z\,\mathbf{k}</m>. Use the Divergence Theorem to
        find the flux of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> across the part of the
        paraboloid <m>x^2 + y^2 + z = <eval obj="h"/></m> that lies above
        the plane <m>z = 1</m> and is oriented upward.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="b" width="6"/>
        (a number)
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{S} \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Write <m>b</m> for the coefficient of <m>z\,\mathbf{k}</m> and
        <m>a^2 = h - 1</m>, so the paraboloid meets the plane <m>z=1</m>
        in the circle of radius <m>a</m>. Then
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = b</m>. Close the surface with the
        disk <m>S_1</m> of radius <m>a</m> at <m>z = 1</m>, oriented
        downward; there <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot(-\mathbf{k}) = -b</m>, so
        <m>\iint_{S_1} = -b\pi a^2</m>. The enclosed volume is
        <m>\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{a}\left(a^2 - r^2\right)r\,dr\,d\theta
        = \tfrac{\pi a^4}{2}</m>, so the closed-surface flux is
        <m>b\,\tfrac{\pi a^4}{2}</m>, and
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = b\,\frac{\pi a^4}{2} - \left(-b\pi a^2\right)
        = b\left(\frac{a^4}{2} + a^2\right)\pi.
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>b = 3</m> and <m>a^2 = 5 - 1 = 4</m>, so the flux is
<m>3\left(\tfrac{2^4}{2} + 2^2\right)\pi = 36\pi \approx 113.10</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 12.  Constant field through a helicoid  (RP3 #12)                    -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-helicoid">
    <title>Flux Through a Helicoid</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="w" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-expression>w/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="integrand" context="formula">
        <de-expression>(w/2)*(sin(u)-cos(u)+v)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>(w/2)*(2+pi/2)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="wrongsign" context="formula">
        <de-expression>(w/2)*(cos(u)-sin(u)-v)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma</m>,
        where the vector field
        <m>\mathbf{F} = <eval obj="c"/>\,\mathbf{i}
        + <eval obj="c"/>\,\mathbf{j} + <eval obj="c"/>\,\mathbf{k}</m>
        and the parametrized surface <m>S</m> is the helicoid
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{r}(u,v) = \left(v\cos u,\ v\sin u,\ u\right),
        \qquad 0 \le u \le \pi, \quad 0 \le v \le 1,
      </me>
      <p>
        oriented upward.
      </p>
      <p>
        First compute the integrand (as an expression in <m>u</m> and
        <m>v</m>):
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\left(\mathbf{r}_v\times\mathbf{r}_u\right) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="integrand" width="20"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <mathcmp obj="wrongsign"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        With <m>\mathbf{r}_u = \left(-v\sin u, v\cos u, 1\right)</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{r}_v = \left(\cos u, \sin u, 0\right)</m>, the upward
        normal is
        <m>\mathbf{r}_v\times\mathbf{r}_u
        = \sin u\,\mathbf{i} - \cos u\,\mathbf{j} + v\,\mathbf{k}</m>.
        Writing <m>c</m> for the common component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = c\int_0^1\!\!\int_0^{\pi}\left(\sin u - \cos u + v\right)
        du\,dv = c\left(2 + \frac{\pi}{2}\right).
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>c = 1</m>, so the flux is
<m>2 + \dfrac{\pi}{2} \approx 3.57</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 13.  Flux through a paraboloid over a disk  (RP3 #13)                -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-paraboloid-updisk">
    <title>Flux Through a Paraboloid, Directly</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="R" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="Rsq" context="number">
        <de-expression>R^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="integrand" context="formula">
        <de-expression>2*a*(x^2+y^2)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>pi*a*R^4</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the flux of the vector field
        <m>\mathbf{F} = <eval obj="a"/>x\,\mathbf{i}
        + <eval obj="a"/>y\,\mathbf{j}</m> through the surface <m>S</m>,
        which is the part of the surface
        <m>z = <eval obj="Rsq"/> - \left(x^2 + y^2\right)</m> above the
        disk of radius <m><eval obj="R"/></m> centered at the origin,
        oriented <alert>upward</alert>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Writing <m>z = f(x,y)</m>, we have
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma = \left(-f_x\,\mathbf{i} - f_y\,\mathbf{j}
        + \mathbf{k}\right)dx\,dy</m>, so (as an expression in <m>x</m>
        and <m>y</m>)
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\left(-f_x\,\mathbf{i} - f_y\,\mathbf{j}
        + \mathbf{k}\right) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="integrand" width="16"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        Flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Writing the surface as <m>z = f(x,y) = R^2 - x^2 - y^2</m> over
        the disk <m>D</m> of radius <m>R</m>,
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma = \left(2x\,\mathbf{i} + 2y\,\mathbf{j} +
        \mathbf{k}\right)dx\,dy</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma = 2a\left(x^2+y^2\right)dx\,dy</m>,
        where <m>a</m> is the coefficient in <m>\mathbf{F}</m>. In polar
        coordinates,
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{Flux} = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{R} 2a\,r^3\,dr\,d\theta
        = \pi a R^4.
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 2</m> and <m>R = 3</m>, so the flux is
<m>\pi(2)\left(3^4\right) = 162\pi \approx 508.94</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 14.  Green's theorem on a clockwise parallelogram  (RP3 #14)         -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <figure xml:id="fig-r3p-parallelogram">
    <caption>
      The parallelogram of <xref ref="ex-r3p-parallelogram"/>, with its
      orientation. Note the direction of the arrows.
    </caption>
    <image width="55%">
      <shortdescription>
      A parallelogram anchored at the origin with arrows showing its boundary orientation.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-r3p-parallelogram">
        <diagram dimensions="(360,240)" margins="10">
          <coordinates bbox="(-0.8,-0.55,4.9,2.3)">
            <axes at="pg-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
            <polygon at="pg-par" closed="yes" stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"
                fill="#CC0079" fill-opacity="0.15"
                points="((0,0),(2,1.4),(4,1.4),(2,0))"/>
            <vector at="pg-e1" v="(0.7,0.49)" tail="(0.75,0.525)"
                stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="pg-e2" v="(0.8,0)" tail="(2.7,1.4)"
                stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="pg-e3" v="(-0.7,-0.49)" tail="(3.35,0.945)"
                stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
            <vector at="pg-e4" v="(-0.8,0)" tail="(1.3,0)"
                stroke="#CC0079" thickness="3"/>
            <point at="pg-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="sw">
              <m>(0,0)</m>
            </point>
            <point at="pg-B" p="(2,0)" fill="black" alignment="se">
              <m>(x_0,0)</m>
            </point>
            <point at="pg-T1" p="(2,1.4)" fill="black" alignment="nw">
              <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>
            </point>
            <point at="pg-T2" p="(4,1.4)" fill="black" alignment="ne">
              <m>(2x_0,y_0)</m>
            </point>
          </coordinates>

          <annotations>
            <annotation ref="figure" text="A parallelogram with vertices at the origin, at x nought comma zero, at two x nought comma y nought, and at x nought comma y nought. Arrows on the edges show the boundary traversed clockwise: up the left edge from the origin, rightward across the top, down the right edge, and leftward along the bottom back to the origin.">
              <annotation ref="pg-par" text="The parallelogram with vertices at the origin, x nought comma zero, two x nought comma y nought, and x nought comma y nought."/>
              <annotation ref="pg-e1" text="An arrow on the left edge pointing up and to the right, from the origin toward x nought comma y nought."/>
              <annotation ref="pg-e2" text="An arrow on the top edge pointing to the right."/>
              <annotation ref="pg-e3" text="An arrow on the right edge pointing down and to the left, toward x nought comma zero."/>
              <annotation ref="pg-e4" text="An arrow on the bottom edge pointing to the left, back toward the origin. Altogether the circulation is clockwise."/>
            </annotation>
          </annotations>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-parallelogram">
    <title>Work Around a Parallelogram</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="n" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="A" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="x0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="y0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="curl" context="number">
        <de-expression>A</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="area" context="number">
        <de-expression>x0*y0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="work" context="number">
        <de-expression>-A*x0*y0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="poswork" context="number">
        <de-expression>A*x0*y0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use Green's Theorem to evaluate the work done by
        <m>\mathbf{F} = \langle x^{<eval obj="n"/>},\
        <eval obj="A"/>x \rangle</m> in moving an object around the
        boundary of the parallelogram in
        <xref ref="fig-r3p-parallelogram"/>
        (<alert>note the orientation</alert>), with
        <m>x_0 = <eval obj="x0"/></m> and <m>y_0 = <eval obj="y0"/></m>.
        Assume that the object circulates only once around the boundary
        and that its motion begins at the origin and ends at the same
        point.
      </p>
      <p>
        With <m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m>, first
        compute (a number):
        <m>\dfrac{\partial N}{\partial x}
        - \dfrac{\partial M}{\partial y} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="curl" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        The area of the parallelogram is
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="area" width="6"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Work <m>=</m> <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="work" width="10"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp object="poswork" min="poswork.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(poswork.value({})))" max="poswork.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(poswork.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        With <m>M = x^{n}</m> and <m>N = Ax</m>,
        <m>N_x - M_y = A</m>. For the counterclockwise orientation
        Green's Theorem would give <m>A\cdot\text{Area} = A x_0 y_0</m>;
        the boundary in the figure is traversed clockwise, so
        <m>\text{Work} = -A x_0 y_0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>A = 5</m>, <m>x_0 = 3</m> and <m>y_0 = 2</m>, so the work is
<m>-5(3)(2) = -30</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 15.  Three gradient fields and the unit circle  (RP3 #15)            -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-three-fields">
    <title>Three Gradient Fields and the Unit Circle</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a1" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="a2" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="a3" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="fpot" context="formula">
        <de-expression>a1*x*y</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="hpot" context="formula">
        <de-expression>a3*sqrt(x^2+y^2)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="zero" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="Gint" context="number">
        <de-expression>-2*pi*a2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="posGint" context="number">
        <de-expression>2*pi*a2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        (a) Each of the vector fields
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \mathbf{F} \amp = <eval obj="a1"/>y\,\mathbf{i}
          + <eval obj="a1"/>x\,\mathbf{j},
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \mathbf{G} \amp = \frac{<eval obj="a2"/>y}{x^2+y^2}\,\mathbf{i}
          + \frac{-<eval obj="a2"/>x}{x^2+y^2}\,\mathbf{j},
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \mathbf{H} \amp = \frac{<eval obj="a3"/>x}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}
          \,\mathbf{i}
          + \frac{<eval obj="a3"/>y}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\,\mathbf{j}
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        is a gradient vector field on some domain (not necessarily the
        whole plane). Find potential functions for <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{H}</m>, choosing the constant so that the potential is
        <m>0</m> at the origin; and find the constant <m>C</m> for the
        potential of <m>\mathbf{G}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>\mathbf{F}</m>: <m>f(x,y) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="fpot" width="16"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>\mathbf{G}</m>: <m>g(x,y)
        = C\arctan\!\left(\dfrac{x}{y}\right)</m> with <m>C =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="a2" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>\mathbf{H}</m>: <m>h(x,y) =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="hpot" width="16"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) Find the line integrals of <m>\mathbf{F}</m>,
        <m>\mathbf{G}</m>, <m>\mathbf{H}</m> around the curve <m>C</m>
        given to be the unit circle in the <m>xy</m>-plane, centered at
        the origin, and traversed counterclockwise.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} =</m>
        <fillin name="b4" mode="math" ansobj="zero" width="8"/>,
        <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{G}\cdot d\mathbf{r} =</m>
        <fillin name="b5" mode="math" ansobj="Gint" width="8"/>,
        <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{H}\cdot d\mathbf{r} =</m>
        <fillin name="b6" mode="math" ansobj="zero" width="8"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) For which of the three vector fields can Green's Theorem be
        used to calculate the line integral in part (b)? Type the letters
        of all that apply, with no spaces (for example <c>fg</c>):
        <fillin name="b7" mode="string" answer="f" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>(Be sure that you are able to explain why or why not.)</em>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp object="posGint" min="posGint.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(posGint.value({})))" max="posGint.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(posGint.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <strcmp case="insensitive">f</strcmp>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp case="insensitive">fgh</strcmp>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp strip="no">.*</strcmp>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        (a) <m>f(x,y) = a_1 xy</m>,
        <m>g(x,y) = a_2\arctan\!\left(x/y\right)</m> (so <m>C = a_2</m>,
        valid where <m>y \ne 0</m>), and
        <m>h(x,y) = a_3\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> (valid except at the origin),
        where <m>a_1, a_2, a_3</m> are the coefficients in
        <m>\mathbf{F}</m>, <m>\mathbf{G}</m>, <m>\mathbf{H}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) <m>\int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} = 0</m> since
        <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is a gradient field on the whole plane and
        <m>C</m> is closed. On the unit circle <m>\mathbf{G}</m> is
        tangent, of length <m>a_2</m>, pointing clockwise, so
        <m>\int_C \mathbf{G}\cdot d\mathbf{r} = -2\pi a_2</m>.
        <m>\mathbf{H}</m> is radial, hence perpendicular to <m>C</m>, so
        its integral is <m>0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) Only <m>\mathbf{F}</m>: the domains of <m>\mathbf{G}</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{H}</m> do not include the origin, which is inside
        <m>C</m>, so Green's Theorem does not apply to them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>a_1 = 2</m>, <m>a_2 = 3</m> and <m>a_3 = 4</m>, so
<m>f = 2xy</m>, <m>C = 3</m>, <m>h = 4\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>, and the three
line integrals are <m>0</m>, <m>-6\pi \approx -18.85</m>, and <m>0</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 16.  Circulation around a rectangle  (RP3 #16)                       -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-rect-circulation">
    <title>Circulation Around a Rectangle</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="x0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="y0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="curlF" context="formula">
        <de-expression>-a*x</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="circ" context="number">
        <de-expression>-a*x0^2*y0/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="posax" context="formula">
        <de-expression>a*x</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use Green's Theorem to calculate the circulation of
        <m>\mathbf{F} = <eval obj="a"/>xy\,\mathbf{i}</m> around the
        rectangle <m>0 \le x \le <eval obj="x0"/></m>,
        <m>0 \le y \le <eval obj="y0"/></m>, oriented counterclockwise.
      </p>
      <p>
        With <m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m>, first
        compute (as an expression in <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>):
        <m>\dfrac{\partial N}{\partial x}
        - \dfrac{\partial M}{\partial y} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="curlF" width="10"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        circulation <m>=</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="circ" width="10"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <mathcmp obj="posax"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        With <m>M = axy</m> and <m>N = 0</m>,
        <m>N_x - M_y = -ax</m>, so by Green's Theorem
      </p>
      <me>
        \oint_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \int_0^{x_0}\!\!\int_0^{y_0} (-ax)\,dy\,dx
        = -\frac{a\,x_0^2\,y_0}{2}.
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 2</m>, <m>x_0 = 4</m> and <m>y_0 = 3</m>, so the circulation is
<m>-\dfrac{2\left(4^2\right)(3)}{2} = -48</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 17.  Circulation around a circle of radius m  (RP3 #17)              -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-circle-green">
    <title>Circulation Around a Circle</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="c1" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c2" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c4" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="u" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="0" max="1" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="d0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c3" context="number">
        <de-expression>c2+(2*u-1)*d0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="m" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="diff" context="number">
        <de-expression>(2*u-1)*d0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="circ" context="number">
        <de-expression>(2*u-1)*d0*pi*m^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate
        <m>\displaystyle\int_C \left(\left(<eval obj="c1"/>x
        + <eval obj="c2"/>y\right)\mathbf{i}
        + \left(<eval obj="c3"/>x + <eval obj="c4"/>y\right)\mathbf{j}
        \right)\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>, where <m>C</m> is the circular path
        with center <m>(a,b)</m> and radius <m><eval obj="m"/></m>,
        oriented counterclockwise. Use Green's Theorem.
      </p>
      <p>
        With <m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j}</m>, first
        compute (a number):
        <m>\dfrac{\partial N}{\partial x}
        - \dfrac{\partial M}{\partial y} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="diff" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="circ" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        <m>N_x - M_y</m> is the constant difference of the two cross
        coefficients, so Green's Theorem gives
        <m>\left(N_x - M_y\right)\cdot\pi m^2</m>, where <m>m</m> is the
        radius; the center <m>(a,b)</m> plays no role.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>N_x - M_y = 7 - 4 = 3</m> and <m>m = 3</m>, so the integral is
<m>3\pi\left(3^2\right) = 27\pi \approx 84.82</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 18.  Cylinder between two slanted planes  (RP3 #18)                  -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-cylinder-band">
    <title>Flux Through a Slanted Cylinder Band</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="R" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="Rsq" context="number">
        <de-expression>R^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="z0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="dz" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="z1" context="number">
        <de-expression>z0+dz</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>2*pi*R^2*dz</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the flux of
        <m>\mathbf{F} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
        through the surface of the cylinder
        <m>x^2 + y^2 = <eval obj="Rsq"/></m> bounded below by the plane
        <m>x + y + z = <eval obj="z0"/></m>, above by the plane
        <m>x + y + z = <eval obj="z1"/></m>, and
        <alert>oriented away from the <m>z</m>-axis</alert>. Note that we
        are only interested in the flux through the curved surface of the
        cylinder.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the cylinder, <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="R" width="6"/>
        (a number).
      </p>
      <p>
        Flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        On the cylinder of radius <m>R</m> the outward normal is
        <m>\langle x, y, 0\rangle / R</m>, so
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \left(x^2+y^2\right)/R = R</m>: the
        <m>z</m>-component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is tangent to the surface.
        The two slanted planes cut every vertical line on the cylinder in
        a segment of the same length <m>\Delta z = z_1 - z_0</m>, so the
        band has area <m>2\pi R\,\Delta z</m>, and
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{Flux} = R\cdot 2\pi R\,\Delta z = 2\pi R^2\,\Delta z.
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>R = 2</m> and <m>\Delta z = 4 - 1 = 3</m>, so the flux is
<m>2\pi\left(2^2\right)(3) = 24\pi \approx 75.40</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  </exercises>
</section>
